<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Prasad Bismil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bismil, Pandit Ram Prasad Bismil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ram Prasad Bismil was an Indian poet, writer and revolutionary against British rule who wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi under the pen names Ram, Agyat and Bismil. Born in Shahjahanpur district in the North-Western Provinces, he formed the revolutionary group Matrivedi and took part in the Mainpuri Conspiracy of 1918, then lived in hiding. A founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> Republican Association, he planned the Kakori train robbery of 9 August 1925, in which ten revolutionaries stopped a train near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> to seize the government treasury it carried. After 18 months of legal process he was sentenced to death and hanged at Gorakhpur Jail on 19 December 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-prasad-bismil.jpg" alt="Ram Prasad Bismil" width="183" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Krantmlverma at Hindi Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Accused_of_Cacori_Conspiracy1271.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ram Prasad Bismil was born on 11 June 1897 to Muralidhar and Moolmati Devi in a Tomar Rajput family. He learned Hindi from his father and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> from a moulvi, entered an English-language school against his father's wishes and joined the Arya Samaj in Shahjahanpur, where Swami Dayananda Saraswati's Satyarth Prakash inspired him.</p><p>Bismil was his pen name, meaning wounded, and Pandit an honorific for his learning. As a student of eighteen he wrote the Hindi poem Mera Janm after the death sentence on Bhai Parmanand and showed it to Swami Somdev. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> he and friends published a book on American independence under a fictitious author's name, and the government of the province banned it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bismil formed the group Matrivedi with the teacher Genda Lal Dixit, whose own youth organisation was called Shivaji Samiti. On 28 January 1918 he distributed the pamphlet Deshvasiyon Ke Nam Sandesh with his poem Mainpuri Ki Pratigya, and the group raised money through three robberies in 1918. When police closed in he escaped by swimming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> underwater, and the Mainpuri Conspiracy case ended on 1 November 1919 with Bismil and Dixit declared absconders.</p><p>He lived in hiding, publishing books including the poems Man Ki Lahar and translations from Bengali. After the Gaya session of 1922 he and other young men broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> over the end of non-co-operation, and in 1923 he drafted the party's constitution, which a meeting at Cawnpore on 3 October 1924 named the Hindustan Republican Association.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Over 40 revolutionaries were arrested after the Kakori train robbery, although only ten had taken part, and one passenger, Ahmed Ali, was accidentally shot dead. Bismil, Ashfaqulla Khan, Roshan Singh and Rajendra Nath Lahiri were sentenced to death after 18 months of legal process, and an appeal to the Privy Council on 16 September 1927 failed. Lahiri was hanged two days earlier and Bismil at Gorakhpur Jail. He wrote his autobiography as a condemned prisoner, and it was published in 1928 as Kakori ke shaheed. His memorials include a statue at Shahjahanpur, a railway station named for him and a postage stamp issued on 19 December 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ram Prasad Bismil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poem was popularised by <b>Ram Prasad Bismil</b>, an Indian freedom fighter, who made it a war cry against British rule; when he was put on the gallows, its opening lines were on his lips.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shahjahanpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Gorakhpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.88,79.91&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Shahjahanpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6646 1142 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ram Prasad Bismil"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7096 1412h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7096 1412h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.88,79.91&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
